i, Song,d1932-106880-02aZhongguo qing tong qi.lEnglish10aChinese bronze ware /cLi Song.  aUpdated ed.  aCambridge, UK ;aNew York :bCambridge University Press,c2011.  a156 pages :billustrations (chiefly color) ;c23 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aIntroductions to Chinese culture  aOriginal ed. published: Beijing : China Intercontinental Press.0 aBronze age -- The world of bronze ware -- Beauty of patterns : from legend to reality -- History recorded in inscriptions -- Convergence and pluralism -- Brilliant sunset glow -- Discovering, collecting and studying bronze ware.  aIngrambShelf ready books 0aBronzes, ChineseyTo 221 B.C. 0aBronzes, ChineseyQin-Han dynasties, 221 B.C.-220 A.D. 0aIntroductions to Chinese culture.1 6100-01a$1!Cs!D-(B,d1932-106240-02a$1!04K7oK_p']\!7I(BlEnglish  a92bTNT  aNK7983.22 .L46813 2011wLCc1i50706010908821f6/11/2025g2lSTACKSmERCp$21.59rYsYtBOOKu11/25/2019xBOOK03939cam  2200553   u548658SIRSI20191123062208.1990611s2000    enka     b    001 0deng    a   99036098  aDLCbengcDLCdUKMdLHAdBAKERdMUQdNLGGCdBTCTAdYDXCPdITJCUdUBCdHEBISdZWZdBDXdOCLCOdOCLCAdUPXdOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLCQdZLMdDHAdOCLCQdOCLCOdYDXdUKMGBdSNNdOCLCQdBGUdOCLCOdCPSdOCLCQdOCLCA  c(S  aGBA0126352bnb  a0415046181q(pbk.)  a9780415046183q(pbk.)  a(OCoLC)4158059500aDF235b.S54 2000  aMAIN1 aShipley, Graham.14aThe Greek world after Alexander, 323-30 B.C. /cGraham Shipley.  aLondon ;aNew York :bRoutledge,c2000.  axxxi, 568 pages :billustrations ;c25 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aRoutledge history of the ancient world  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 475-536) and index.0 aApproaches and sources -- Alexander and his successors to 276 B.C. -- Kings and cities -- Macedonia and Greece -- Religion and philosophy -- Ptolemaic Egypt -- Literature and social identity -- The Seleukid kingdom and Pergamon -- Understanding the cosmos : Greek 'science' after Aristotle -- Rome and Greece.1 a"In this comprehensive and well-documented book, Graham Shipley integrates the diverse aspects of politics, society and culture to create a coherent and thorough survey of the Hellenistic world. The Greek World after Alexander examines social changes in the cities of the Greek world and in the kingdoms that succeeded Alexander's empire. The investigation is set in the context of an up-to-date appraisal of the momentous military and political changes that took place after Alexander's reign. Graham Shipley's ground-breaking study also considers developments in literature, religion, philosophy, and science, establishing whether they departed radically from Classical Greek culture or developed continuously from it. In addition, he explores the divisions in Hellenistic culture separating an educated ‚elite from the general population which was more mobile than before but perhaps less involved in city politics. The Greek World after Alexander offers an indispensable introduction to the Hellenistic world, and provides the reader with extensive translated source material and references for further study. It will be invaluable to students, teachers and researchers alike."--Publisher's description.  aIngrambShelf ready books006880-01aAlexander,cthe Great,d356 B.C.-323 B.C.xInfluence.04aAlexander,bIII,cKing of MacedoniaxInfluence. 0aHellenismxHistoriography. 06880-02aGreecexHistoryyTo 146 B.C.xHistoriography. 0aGreecexHistoryyMacedonian Hegemony, 323-281 B.C.xHistoriography. 0aRomexHistoryyRepublic, 265-30 B.C.xHistoriography. 0aMediterranean RegionxCivilizationxGreek influences. 7aCivilizationxGreek influences. 7aHistoriography. 7aInfluence (Literary, artistic, etc.) 7aGreece. 7aMediterranean Region. 7aRome (Empire) 0aRoutledge history of the ancient world.146600-01/(Sa(