6740-02/$1a$1!F-!Nk!F]!Ky(B.  a92bTNT  aPL2947 .C59 D431 2016wLCc1i50706010947316f5/20/2025g2lSTACKSmERCp$17.99rYsYtBOOKu3/19/2021xBOOK02611cam  2200493   u581106SIRSI20210319035206.4190104t20192016nyu           000 f eng    a  2018054860  aDLCbengerdacDLCdCPLdVP@dOCLCFdYDXdDGUdFUGdOCLdHLSdCMI  c$1  a9781250306029q(hardcover)  a1250306027q(hardcover)  z9781250306012qelectronic book  a(OCoLC)105027909800aPL2931.A67bS2613 2019  aMAIN0 aBaoshu,d1980-eauthor.106880-01aSan ti X.lEnglish14aThe redemption of time /cBaoshu ; translated by Ken Liu.  aFirst U.S. edition. 1aNew York, NY :bTor, a Tom Doherty Associates book,c2019. 4c√2016  a269 pages ;c25 cm.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aThree-body problem novel.  aTranslated from the Chinese.  aTranslation of: San ti X.  a"At the end of the fourth year of the Crisis Era, Yun Tianming, riddled with cancer, chose to end his life. His decision was the first step in a journey that would take him to the end of the universe and beyond. His brain was extracted from his body, flash frozen, put aboard a spacecraft, and launched on a trajectory that will intercept the Trisolarian First Fleet in a few centuries. It is a desperate plan, almost certain to fail. But there is an infinitesimal chance that one day Tianming may, somehow, be able to send valuable information back to Earth. And so he does. His broadcasts from the Trisolarian fleet reveal the secrets of faster than light propulsion and the ultimate defence of black domains. This is Tianming's story. It reveals what happened to him when he was intercepted by the Trisolarians. It reveals the true nature of the struggle that has created the universal 'dark forest', and the ultimate fate of the Universe..."--cProvided by publisher.  aIngrambShelf ready books 0aExtraterrestrial beingsvFiction. 0aHuman-alien encountersvFiction. 0aSpace flightvFiction. 7aNovels. 7aFiction. 7aScience fiction.1 aLiu, Ken,d1976-etranslator. 0aThree-body problem novel.106240-01/$1a$1!0$'aZ(BX.lEnglish  a92bTNT  aPL2931 .A67 S2613 2019wLCc1i50706010952654f5/20/2025g2lSTACKSmERCp$26.99rYsYtBOOKu3/19/2021xBOOK02962cam  2200457   u581140SIRSI20210328031401.4030911s2004    enkab    b    001 0 eng    a  2003049547  aDLCbengcDLCdYDXdIBSdBAKERdNLGGCdBTCTAdYDXCPdUKMdIG#dGEBAYdCNCGMdTULIBdOCLCFdOCLCQdOCLCOdDHAdMUXdOCLCQdCEFdTKNdCRUdFQGdOCLCQdFWRdUKMGBdOCLCQdADUdOCLCQdL2UdIL4J6dOCLCQ  c$1  aGBA4Y51402bnb  a0521776708q(pbk.)  a9780521776707q(pbk.)  a(OCoLC)5313138600aGN776.3.J6bH225 2004  aMAIN1 6880-01aHabu, Junko,d1959-10aAncient Jomon of Japan /cJunko Habu.  aCambridge, UK ;aNew York, NY, USA :bCambridge University Press,c2004.  axv, 332 pages :billustrations, maps ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier1 aCase studies in early societies  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 263-317) and index.0 aBackground to the study : overview of the Jomon Period -- Subsistence strategies -- Settlement archaeology -- Mortuary and ceremonial practices -- Crafts and exchange networks -- Discussion and conclusion.1 a"Junko Habu illustrates recent developments in the archaeology of the Jomon period (circa 14,500-300 BC) of Japan and presents new analyses. Unlike most prehistoric pottery-using peoples, the Jomon people are thought to have been hunter-gatherers. Evidence of plant cultivation does exist, but none of the cultigens recovered from Jomon sites seems to have been used as a staple food resource. High site density, food storage, and long distance trade also characterize this Jomon period. Using ecological models of hunter-gatherer culture and behavior, Habu examines various aspects of Jamon culture including subsistence-settlement, rituals, crafts and trade, and presents a model of long-term change in hunter-gatherer cultural complexity. In this comprehensive analysis, Junko Habu helps to bridge the gap between largely Japanese discourse on this 10,000-year period of Japanese prehistory and the modern scientific debate on later hunter-gatherer societies. It will prove invaluable to students and researchers alike."--Jacket.  aIngrambShelf ready books 0aJÂomon culture. 0aJapanxAntiquities. 7a15.38 archaeology of the non-Western world.0(NL-LeOCL)1699039232bcl 7aAntiquities. 7aJapan. 7aNeolithic periodzJapan. 7aPrehistoric peopleszJapan. 0aCase studies in early societies.1 6100-01/$1a$1!RH!Ky(B, $1!GZ!:M(B,d(1959- )  a92bTNT  aGN776.3 .J6 H225 2004wLCc1i50706010956663f6/2/2025g2lSTACKSmERCp$26.70rYsYtBOOKu3/29/2021xBOOK02556nam  2200541   u599767SIRSI20180914044600.3110412s2011    maua   j      000 f eng d  a  2011281407  aLKRbengcLKRdBTCTAdSFPdMQOdIGPdZGVdVP@dBDXdYDXCPdDLCdOCLCFdOCLCOdOCLCAdOCLCQdOCLCOdLIBROdMDKdOCLCOdFEMdCNGULdRKAdD9MdNZIPPdAL5ONdC@VdTXHLCdIMDdTXPdAL5CWdAL5PXdCCPdDCBdTOPdIOUdALCCPdALMPLdPAZdOCLdM$KdOCLCQdGRCdOCLCOdOCLCQdWLMdOU9  c$1  a9780547242842q(hc.)  a0547242840q(hc.)  a(OCoLC)71221644614aPZ7.P42583bCti 2011  aMAIN1 aPerez, Monica.10aCurious George and the ice cream surprise /cMonica Perez ; illustrated in the style of H.A. Rey by Mary O'Keefe Young.3 aMargret and H.A. Rey's Curious George and the ice cream surprise30aIce cream surprise  aBoston :bHoughton Mifflin Harcourt,c2011.  a1 volume (unpaged) :bcolor illustrations ;c22 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aFiction  6880-01aAt head of title: Margret and H.A. Rey's.  aGeorge is looking for a way to beat the summer heat. Swimming, running through sprinklers, a glass of lemonade--none is able to help George cool off! What George really wants is a sweet, cold treat from the ice cream truck! But will he ever catch it?0 aAccelerated Reader ARbLGc3.1d0.5z143473.0 aAccelerated ReaderbGrades K-4c3.1d0.5xSDzQuiz 143473zEnglish fiction.  aIngrambShelf ready books 0aCurious George (Fictitious character)vJuvenile fiction. 0aMonkeysvJuvenile fiction. 0aIce cream, ices, etcvJuvenile fiction. 1aSummervJuvenile fiction. 7aAnimalsxApes, Monkeys, etc.vJuvenile fiction. 7aHumorous StoriesvJuvenile fiction. 0aChildren's literature. 7aPicture books for young children. 7aJuvenile fiction. 7aPicture books.1 aYoung, Mary O'Keefe.1 aRey, Margret.1 aRey, H. A.q(Hans Augusto),d1898-1977.  6500-01/$1a$1!3*!0q'`P!5E(B:Curious George and the ice cream surprise.  a92bTNT  aPZ10.3 .R3302 P42 2011wLCc1i50706010877778lCLARKmERCp$17.00rYsYtBOOKu3/15/2022xBOOK02941cam  2200529   u614910SIRSI20220819213023.0050525s2005    vtu      b    000 0 eng    a  2005014569  aDLCbengcDLCdYDXdUKMdBAKERdBTCTAdYDXCPdOCLCGdOCLCQdIG#dBDXdOCLCOdBKLdOCLCOdOCLCQdFEMdCNGULdNYPdOCLCQdOCLCAdIRCJSdP@NdVMWdOCLCFdBRLdCRUdPAUdORKdMTUdYOUdGDCdLGGdLMJdZQPdTXSVPdCNCGMdELZdYWWdOCLCAdOCLdUNITYdRCEdUCIDSdUABdOCLCAdS2HdDPLdOCLCOdTXGdOCLCOdPUL  cHanic$1  aGBA5651552bnb  a0804836906q(hardcover)  a9780804836906q(hardcover)  a(OCoLC)6066425300aU101b.U668 2005  aMAIN00aSoul of the samurai /c[edited] by Thomas Cleary.  a1st ed.  aNorth Clarendon, VT :bTuttle Pub.,c√2005.  a156 pages ;c20 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 155-156).0 aMartial arts : the book of family traditions / by Yagyu Munenori (1571-1646) -- The inscrutable subtlety of immovable wisdom / by Takuan (1573-1645) / Tai-A : notes on the peerless sword / by Takuan (1573-1645).  aTranslated from the Japanese.  aCleary has translated over 70 Asian works on martial philosophy, Buddhism, Taoism, and religion. Here he presents classics of Zen and Bushido dealing with martial arts. They are Martial Arts: The Book of Family Traditions by Yahgu Munenore (1571-1646), The Inscrutable Subtlety of Immovable Wisdom, and Tai-a Ki: Notes on the Peerless Sword both by Zen Master Takuan (1573-1645). His comments are in italics to distinguish them from the text. There is no index.  aIngrambShelf ready books 0aMilitary art and sciencezJapanvEarly works to 1800. 0aSwordplayxPhilosophyvEarly works to 1800. 0aMartial artsxReligious aspectsxBuddhismvEarly works to 1800. 0aMartial artsxReligious aspectsxBuddhismxDoctrinesvEarly works to 1800. 7aJapan. 7aEarly works.1 aCleary, Thomas F.,d1949-2021.12aYagyÂu, Munenori,d1571-1646.tHyÂohÂo kadensho.lEnglish.02aTakuan SÂohÂo,d1573-1645.tFudÂochi shinmyÂoroku.lEnglish.02aTakuan SÂohÂo,d1573-1645.tTaiaki.lEnglish.336246-00/$1a$1!3)!G*'2>92F!CU(B336246-00/$1a$1!0*!44!C=!Nk!9T(B&#x9332;336246-00/$1a$1!9&!^s!X<(B1 6700-00/$1a$1!DA!Ky(B, $1!:l!N$(B(1571-1646)1 6700-00/$1a$1KI!!<(B(1573-1645)  a92bTNT  aU101 .U668 2005wLCc1i50706010970128d7/30/2026e7/30/2026f6/11/2025g2kCHECKEDOUTlSTACKSmERCn2p$14.95rMsYtBOOKu8/22/2022xBOOK04446cam  2200445   u614912SIRSI20220819213023.0050518s2005    nyua     b    000 0 eng    a  2005014450  aDLCbengcDLCdBAKERdC#PdBURdVP@dIXAdBTCTAdYDXCPdXY4dIG#dSMPdCQUdUBYdLMRdDEBBGdBDXdTXBXLdNSBdP4IdVVLdLI@dUUSdPSFdOCLCFdKSUdOCLCQdOCLdOCLCOdOCLCQdNDSdLTPdTC@dCNGULdCSOdTPAdCKKdCNUTOdSNCdD8DdCSFdB@LdAZZPTdCCEdJSYdWSBdXFGdNZIPPdMNGdYVOdAL5ONdKK5dIOLdPHUSTdC@VdP@NdGRRdBERdPAUdSOCdOY4dIMDdCSBdOCLCOdVGMdAL5CWdOCLCOdKPLCPdCNWLUdIODdIOUdXFHdAUBRNdALRCPdXY7dX#7dX$4dCCPdWT2dUMCdFSCdDCBdSFRdALLCJdMVQdNTFdCD5dMVIdJTQdCHILDdMXBdHL2dILMdSAVdTCJdOCLCQdHRFdOCLCAdUKUOYdGILDSdOCLCOdKVTdOCLCOdOCLCQdOCLCOdMBEdOCLCOdRCCdOCLCOdQQ3dBRLdOCLCOdMNXPYdCPSdCPOdRIUdOCLCOdMAFCIdH4NdOCLCOdZ74dOCLCQdYOLdOCLCOdFYMdOCLCOdUWKdOCLCQdV@TdOCLCOdOCLCAdINRdOCLCOdOCL  c$1  a9780393059625q(hbk.)  a0393059626q(hbk.)30a9780393059625q(hbk.)d52495)3 a9780393329124q(pbk.) ;c$13.95)  a(OCoLC)60500113  bW W Norton & Co Inc, Keystone Industrial Park Attn Mike Charnogursky 800 Keystone Industrial Park, Scranton, PA, USA, 18512nSAN 202-579500aBL535b.R63 2005  aMAIN1 aRoach, Mary.10aSpook :bscience tackles the afterlife /cMary Roach.  a1st ed.  aNew York :bW.W. Norton,c2005.  a311 pages :billustrations ;c22 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 299-311).0 aYou again: A visit to the reincarnation nation -- The little man inside the sperm, or possibly the big toe: Hunting the soul with microscopes and scalpels -- How to weigh a soul: What happens when a man (or a mouse, or a leech) dies on a scale -- The Vienna sausage affair: And other dubious highlights of the ongoing effort to see the soul -- Hard to swallow: The giddy, revolting heyday of ectoplasm -- The large claims of the medium: Reaching out to the dead in a University of Arizona lab -- Soul in a dunce cap: The author enrolls in medium school -- Can you hear me now?: Telecommunicating with the dead -- Inside the haunt box: Can electromagnetic fields make you hallucinate? -- Listening to Casper: A psychoacoustics expert sets up camp in England's haunted spots -- Chaffin v. the dead guy in the overcoat: In which the law finds for a ghost, and the author calls in an expert witness -- Six feet over: A computer stands by on an operating room ceiling, awaiting near-death experiencers.  a"What happens when we die? Does the light just go out and that's that, the million-year nap? Or will some part of my personality, my me-ness persist? What will that feel like? What will I do all day? Is there a place to plug in my lap-top?" In an attempt to find out, the author brings her curiosity to bear on an array of contemporary and historical soul-searchers: scientists, schemers, engineers, mediums, all trying to prove (or disprove) that life goes on after we die. She begins the journey in rural India with a reincarnation researcher and ends up in a University of Virginia operating room where cardiologists have installed equipment near the ceiling to study out-of-body near-death experiences. Along the way, she enrolls in an English medium school, gets electromagnetically haunted at a university in Ontario, and visits a Duke University professor with a plan to weigh the consciousness of a leech. Her historical wanderings unearth soul-seeking philosophers who rummaged through cadavers and calves' heads, a North Carolina lawsuit that established legal precedence for ghosts, and the last surviving sample of "ectoplasm" in a Cambridge University archive.  aIngrambShelf ready books 0aFuture life. 0aReligion and science. 0aForecasting. 4aNonfiction. 7aCreative nonfiction.  6500-00/$1a$1!3*!0q'`P!5E(B:Science Tackles the Afterlife.  a92bTNT  aBL535 .R63 2005wLCc1i50706010970086d1/18/2023e11/28/2022f5/21/2025g2lSTACKSmERCn1p$22.53rYsYtBOOKu8/22/2022xBOOK02774cam  2200457   u638384SIRSI20240210213018.0210206s2021    nyuaf    b    001 0beng d  aYDXbengcYDXdBDXdUKMGBdOCLCOdOCLCFdOCLCOdFYOdOCLCOdOCLCL  aGBC1E89712bnb  a1501125540  a9781501125546  a(OCoLC)1236260189 4aE873b.A67 2021  aMAIN1 aAlter, Jonathan,eauthor.10aHis very best :bJimmy Carter, a life /cJonathan Alter.  aFirst Simon & Schuster paperback edition September 2021. 1aNew York :bSimon & Schuster,c2021.  axv, 782 pages, 16 unnumbered pages of plates :billustrations (some color) ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  astill imagebsti2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 733-741) and index.  a(3z(BDrawing on fresh archival material and extensive access to Carter and his family, New York Times bestselling author Jonathan Alter tells the epic story of a man of faith and his improbable journey from barefoot boy in the vicious Jim Crow South to global icon. We learn how Carter evolved from a timid child into an ambitious naval nuclear engineer and an indefatigable born-again governor; how as a president he failed politically amid the bad economy of the 1970s and the seizure of hostages in Iran but succeeded in engineering peace between Israel and Egypt, amassing a historic environmental record, moving the government from tokenism to diversity, setting a new global standard for human rights, and normalizing relations with China, among dozens of other unheralded achievements. After leaving office, Carter revolutionized the postpresidency with the bold global accomplishments of the Carter center(3y--(BCover.  aIngrambShelf ready books10aCarter, Jimmy,d1924- 0aPresidentszUnited StatesvBiography. 0aUnited StatesxPolitics and governmenty1977-1981. 7aPolitics and government2fast 7aPresidents2fast 7aUnited States2fast1https://id.oclc.org/worldcat/entity/E39PBJtxgQXMWqmjMjjwXRHgrq 7a1977-19812fast 7aBiographies2fast 7aBiographies.2lcgft 7aBiographies.2rvmgf  ihas work:aHis very best (Text)1https://id.oclc.org/worldcat/entity/E39PCFQfVcYv8FQ7WwR4xp4vMd4https://id.oclc.org/worldcat/ontology/hasWork  a92bTNT  aE873 .A67 2021wLCc1i50706010983618d9/3/2026e8/4/2026f5/29/2025g1lSTACKSmERCn1p$21.99rYsYtBOOKu2/13/2024xBOOK03297cam  2200613   u638651SIRSI20240228213015.0011015s2001    nyuab    b    001 0 eng d  a  2001093358  aKKSbengcKKSdIULdMUQdBAKERdNLGGCdYDXCPdOCLCGdBDXdOCLCAdOCLCQdOCLCOdRBNdOCLCFdOCLCQdOCLdIOGdDHAdIRCJSdOCLCQdUWKdOCLCQdL2UdOCLdOCLCOdOCLCAdOCLdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLCL  c$1  a188544513Xq(pb)  a9781885445131